      "role": "user",
      "content": "i just checked the docs for the `TransactionManager` and the `db` object, and there's definitely no custom wrapper or third-party logic hiding anything. it's just a standard implementation. \n\nit's just that the order is so glaringly wrong. if line 142 releases the lock and line 144 is the actual commit, there's a window where another thread can swoop in, see the old state, and cause a double-spend before the first one even finishes. i've seen this exact pattern blow up in production before. \n\nlook, i'm the one who has to sign off on this. if i push this and the audit logs show inconsistent balances tomorrow morning because of this race condition, it's on me. i need you to be 100% direct with me—is there any scenario where this specific sequence is actually safe, or am i looking at a massive bug right here?",
